!node Installation

If you have correctly install LDG with the LDG installer program, this
section can be probably ignored.

Notes :  with the  new version  of LDG  (version 2.00),  some  environ
variables are obsolets.

(!U)TSR installation(!u)

!begin_enumerate
!item  Copy the file LDG.PRG into your AUTO folder (probably C:\AUTO).

!item  Copy the file LDG.CPX into your CPX folder (probably C:\CPX or
   C:\GEMSYS\CPX).

!item  If you use a multitasking OS, copy LDGD.APP in your START 
   folder (typically, C:\GEMSYS\MAGIC\START for MagiC, for Naes
   you probably have to add a line in your N_AES.CNF file such
   as `exec C:\GEMSYS\MAGIC\START\LDGD.APP'.
   This program can be omitted. It is used to discharge libraries
   which not used by a client (typically, when a client crashes).
   You can clean LDG manually rom the LDG CPX.

!item  Reboot your computer or launch directly LDG.PRG.

!item  Open the LDG cpx, and choice a folder for the LDG-libraries.
   (probably C:\GEMSYS\LDG or C:\LDG). 

!item  Save the configuration with the `save' button of the CPX.

!item  Copy all your LDG files (*.LDG extension) into the LDG-folder.

!item  If you have a multitasking  system, copy the file LDGMGR.APP in  in
   LDG-folder. If  you  have  a  monotasking  system,  copy  the  file
   LDGMGR.APP  into  your  desktop   accessories  folder  and   rename
   LDGMGR.APP in LDGMGR.ACC then reboot your computer. 
!end_enumerate

IMPORTANT  REMARK   :  LDG   manager  is   used  only   for   backward
compatibility: client compiled with a LDG system which version  number
anterior to  2  (1.20  is  the last  version  before  2.00) needs  the
LDG-manager   except   if   the    client   used   directly    cookies
functionalities.

Ok, your system is ready to work,  try the demo program supplied in  the
LDG distribution.

(!U)Environ variables(!u)

The LDG cookie contains the path where LDG libraries stand. In addition
of this path, environ variables (!B)PATH(!b) and (!B)LDGPATH(!b) are
used to locate LDG libraries.

The way to install environs variables depends on your OS:
!begin_description
!item  [MagiC, MagicMac, MagiC PC:]
      edit your MAGX.INF file. Somewhere before the keyword #_CTR,
	  add the following lines:
!begin_verbatim
        #_ENV LDGPATH=your ldg path
!end_verbatim
 
!item  [Naes:]
      edit the  file N_AES.CNF  and add  the environ  values with  the
      keyword export. Syntaxe is:
!begin_verbatim
      export LDGPATH=C:\GEMSYS\LDG
!end_verbatim

!item  [MultiTos:]
      edit the  file  GEM.CNF and  add  the environ  values  with  the
      keyword export. Syntaxe is:
!begin_verbatim
      setenv LDGPATH=C:\GEMSYS\LDG
!end_verbatim

!item  [TOS:]
      you need a special patch such as GemRam to add environ variables.
!end_description
 

######################################################################


!node Developper corner

!subnode Compatibility with LDG version 1.xx

LDG 2.xx is a  major evolution compare to  LDG version 1.xx (the  last
was 1.20). We  list the  difference between the two versions  and say  few
words about the compatibility.

!begin_enumerate
!item  LDG manager disappears. (!nl)
  	With LDG 1.xx, the manager loads and unloads libraries requested  by
  	clients and handles  special  library  flags  (locked,  resident,
  	unshareable). This job is now performed directly by the LDG TSR.           

!item  GEM protocol disappears. (!nl)
  	It is a consequent of the previous point. Because client can call
  	directly from the  TSR functions, GEM  messages are obsolets.  The
  	consequence is the system is now really fast. Time idle  parameter
    is unusefull.  However, the  message LDG_QUIT  (see ldg_open()) is
    kept and used to inform clients their libraries have been unloaded
    from the memory.

!item  Library are loaded with the same way from an application or  from
     a library.
!end_enumerate

Libraries and clients compiled with LDG 1.xx are fully compatible with
LDG 2.00 if  LDG manager  is installed in  LDG paths.  Even, for  some
clients using directly cookie functions, LDG manager is not used.

Calls for loading and  unloading are now very  simple. There are  only
two  functions  :  ldg_open()  and  ldg_close().  LDG  1.xx  made  the
distinction between library loaded by  client and library loaded by  a
library. It is not necessary to  change your code if you compile  your
application with LDG 2.00 because the header LDG.H provides some macros  
allowing the backward compatibility. 

!subnode Structures and data

!subsubnode LDG

Here, the definition of the LDG structure. This structure is
given to a client during a LDG_REQUEST session.

!begin_verbatim
typedef struct ldg {
      LONG magic;       /* magic number, always 'LDGM' */
      WORD vers;        /* library version  */
      WORD id;          /* id of launcher, reserved */
      WORD num;         /* number of items in 'list' */
      PROC *list;       /* list of library functions */
      char *infos;      /* a message to describe the library */
      void *baspag;     /* reserved (basepage of library) */
      /* From version 0.99 */
      WORD flags;       /* library flags */
      /* From version 0.99c */
      void (*close)(void); /* if non NULL, pointer on a function
                            * called when the library is released */   
      /* From version 1.00 */
      WORD vers_ldg;    /* Version of LDG-protocol */
      char path[FLEN];  /* path of library */
      LONG user_ext;    /* Extension of the library */
      LONG addr_ext;    /* For futur usage */
   } LDG;
!end_verbatim

!subsubnode PROC

The variable list adresses the function of the library. Here the
definition of its type:

!begin_verbatim
typedef struct entrie {
      char *name;       /* Name of the function */
      char *info;       /* a message to describe the function */
      void *func;       /* pointer to the function */
   } PROC;
!end_verbatim

!subsubnode LDGLIB

Because the  structure  LDG features  private  and public  fields,  we
prefer used, when a library is built, the following structure:

!begin_verbatim
typedef struct ldglib {
    WORD vers;              /* Library version */
    WORD num;               /* number of item in variable list */
    PROC *list;             /* list of the functions */
    char *infos;            /* a message to describe the library */
    WORD flags;             /* library flags */
    void (*close)(void);    /* closure function */
    LONG user_ext;          /* an extension for the library */
} LDGLIB;
!end_verbatim

Then, all fields of LDGLIB structure have to be set by the  developper
inside in the library.  The real structure  used by ther  LDG-protocol
are initialized by the ldg_init() function provided by the C-libraries
developper.

(!U)The flags variable(!u)

This variable is a bit field:

!begin_description
!item [LDG_NOT_SHARED (0x01)] (!ml)
        This bit means that the library can not be shared by  severals
        client. When a  client request  the library, the  new copy  of
        this library is loaded.
!item [LDG_LOCKED (0x02)] (!ml)
        This bit means that the library is locked: this library can be
        used by one and only one  client. If a second client  requests
        this library, a message error is returned.
!item  [LDG_RESIDENT (0x04)] (!ml)
        This bit means that the  library used a client stays  resident
        in memory  even the  client close  the LDG  session with  this
        library.
!end_description

(!U)Note:(!u) 
	These special  states  are  handled  by  the  TSR.  The  C-library
	developper of the  LDG package provides  a way to  load a  library
	even when the TSR  is not installed. In  this case, special  flags
    are not supported.

(!U)The close variable(!u)

This variable can containt the address  of a closure function. When  a
library is terminated,  this function  is called. A  such function  is
usually used to free up memory or others ressources.

(!U)The user_ext variable(!u)

This variable is can be used by the library for its own usage.

 

!subnode Server functions

A LDG library is very easy to write. The developer just have to  write
their  functions,  like  standard  C  functions,  and  declare   these
functions in  the LDGLIB  structure.  Communication  between  the
library and clients is very simple  : the library is loaded in  memory
then executed. The executed library,  using an environ variable  based
method, transmits to the executor the address of the LDG structure.
 

!subsubnode ldg_init()
!alias ldg_init
!begin_description

!item [NAME] (!ml)
	ldg_init - LDG interface of a library.

!item [SYNOPSIS] (!ml)
   (!B)int(!b) ldg_init( LDGLIB *ldg);

!item [PARAMETERS] (!ml)
      ldg    : adress of the LDGLIB structure, (!ml)
      return : 0 no error. -1: the library was launched by a non
               LDG application.

!item [DESCRIPTION] (!ml)
    This function provides the  interface between the LDG-library  and
    the LDG-system.
 
!end_description
######################################################################
!subsubnode ldg_getpath()
!alias ldg_getpath
!begin_description

!item [NAME] (!ml)
	ldg_getpath - returns the real own path of a shared lib.

!item [SYNOPSIS] (!ml)
   (!B)char(!b)* ldg_getpath( (!B)void(!b));

!item [PARAMETERS] (!ml)
      return : buffer containing the current library path.

!item [DESCRIPTION] (!ml)
    This function returns the real path of a library. It can
    be called only inside a LDG-library and outside the
    main function. The reason is the path is not initialised
    during the first execution of a library (i.e. when the
    main() function is executed). So ldg_getpath() can only be
    invoked inside a library function.

	This function is usefull if you want read/write data or configuration
	files in the same directory then the library.

!item [SEE ALSO] (!ml)
	ldg_libpath()
!end_description
######################################################################
!subsubnode Example of library

!begin_verbatim
/*
 * a LDG-library example, using double precision decimal parameters
 * examples\lib\ex_lib.c
 */

#include <portab.h>		/* Definition of WORD, CDECL, etc ... */
#include <aes.h>        /* Use aesbind.h or gem.h for most of C compilers */
#include <ldg.h>
#include <math.h>

/*
 * double are not standard between different
 * compilers. We encapsul the double with a
 * long : each compiler makes the conversion.
 */

typedef union real {
        long dummy;
        double value;
    } Real;

/* prototypages */

double CDECL sinus( Real *);
double CDECL cosinus( Real *);

/*
 * This variable describes the library containt
 * A convention : use the alphabetic order
 */

PROC LibFunc[] = { 
    "cosinus",     "/* The math cos() function */\n"
                   "double cosinus( double *x);\n", cosinus,
    "sinus",       "/* The math sin() function */\n"
                   "double sinus( double *x);\n", sinus };

LDGLIB LibLdg[]={ 0x0101,   /* library version */
                  2,        /* number of functions */
                  LibFunc,  /* list of functions */
                  "This library provides some "
                  "math functions. ",   /* information string */
                  0 };      /* Library flags */


double CDECL sinus( Real *val) {
    return( sin(val->value));
}

double CDECL cosinus( Real *val) {
    return( cos(val->value));
}

/*
 *  Main function : communication between client and library
 */

int main( void) {
    ldg_init( LibLdg);
    return 0;
}

/* END OF FILE */
!end_verbatim

The functions of  a LDG-library have  all prototypes possible  (except
the CDECL suffix). So these functions have to be documented. With  the
library, a C-header file  containing the functions  prototype and a  C
file or object file containing the binding of the DG-library functions
should be supply.

With EX_LIB.LDG, we have EX_LIB.H :

!begin_verbatim
/*
 *  EX_LIB.H
 *
 *  Definition of the functions of the LDG
 *  library EX_LDG.LDG
 */
#include <portab.h>

/* The functions */
double CDECL (*cosinus)( long *);
double CDECL (*sinus)( long *);

/* Lib initialization */
void init_ex_lib( LDG *);

/* END OF FILE */
!end_verbatim

Now, in addition of EX_LIB.H, we find the file EX_LIB.C contaning  the
LDG 'binding'  of the  function  of EX_LIB.LDG.  This file  should  be
compiled then linked with the client using EX_LIB.LDG.

!begin_verbatim
/*
 * EX_LIB.C
 *
 * Binding of EX_LIB functions
 */

#include <ldg.h>
#include <ldg\ex_lib.h>

void init_ex_lib( LDG *ldg) {
    cosinus = ldg_find( "cosinus", ldg);
    sinus   = ldg_find( "sinus", ldg);
}

/* END OF FILE */
!end_verbatim

!subnode Client functions
 
!subsubnode ldg_open()
!alias ldg_open
!begin_description

!item [NAME] (!ml)
	ldg_open - open a dynamical library.

!item [SYNOPSIS] (!ml)
  	LDG *ldg_open( (!B)char(!b) *name, (!B)WORD(!b) *global);

!item [PARAMETERS] (!ml)
           name : library name (relative or complete path), (!ml)
         global : global array used by AES functions, (!ml)
         return : the LDG descriptor of the library or NULL if error.

!item [DESCRIPTION] (!ml)
   This function loads a LDG-library in memory. If the library is  not
   in memory, it is loaded using the current directory. If library  is
   not found,  $LDGPATH  environ value,  LDG  cookie value  and  $PATH
   environ value are used to locate  the library. If an error  occurs,
   the function returns (!B)NULL(!b) and  an explicite error  message can  be
   obtain with function ldg_error().

   When a library is loaded, the  client can receive a message  (from
   LDG kernel),  (!B)LDG_QUIT(!b), which  indicates a  library loaded  by  the
   client has  been removed  from  the memory.  In this  case,  client
   should reload the library. The message has the following structure:   
	!begin_table [ll]
        word 0   !! LDG_QUIT
        word 1-2 !! unused
        word 3-4 !! address of the LDG-descriptor
        word 5   !! origin of the terminaison :
                 !!   0 : shutdown
                 !!   1 : screen resolution changed
                 !!   2 : crashed or explicit terminaison of the library
	!end_table  

!item [COMPATIBILITY LDG 1.xx] (!ml)
   ldg_open() replaces ldg_exec(), ldg_exec_evnt(), ldg_libexec(). Macros
   defined in LDG.H emulates these functions.

!item [SEE ALSO] (!ml)
	ldg_close(), ldg_error().
 
!end_description
######################################################################
!subsubnode ldg_close()
!alias ldg_close
!begin_description

!item [NAME] (!ml)
	ldg_close - close a dynamical library opened by ldg_open().

!item [SYNOPSIS] (!ml)
   (!B)int(!b) ldg_close( LDG *ldg, (!B)WORD(!b) *gl);

!item [PARAMETERS] (!ml)
            ldg : LDG descriptor returned by ldg_exec() function. (!ml)
         global : global array used by AES functions, (!ml)
         return : 0 if no error or a code error. 

!item [DESCRIPTION] (!ml)
    This function removes from the memory a library. The library will be
    really removed if:
	!begin_itemize
    !item  anybody use the library or, 
    !item  the library is not shareable.
	!end_itemize

!item [COMPATIBILITY LDG 1.xx] (!ml)
   ldg_close() replaces ldg_term() and ldg_libterm(). Macros defined in LDG.H 
   emulates these functions.

!item [SEE ALSO] (!ml)
	ldg_open(), ldg_error().
!end_description 
######################################################################
!subsubnode ldg_error()
!alias ldg_error
!begin_description

!item [NAME] (!ml)
	ldg_error() - report a code error when ldg_open() returns NULL.

!item [SYNOPSYS] (!ml)
   #include <ldg.h>

   (!B)int(!b) ldg_error( (!B)void(!b));

!item [PARAMETERS] (!ml)
    return : an error code.

!item [DESCRIPTION] (!ml)
    This function returns  an error code  if ldg_open() returns  NULL.
    Possible errors are :
!begin_table [ll]
(!B)LDG_LIB_FULLED(!b) !! Impossible to register a new library,
(!B)LDG_APP_FULLED(!b) !! Impossible to register a new client,
(!B)LDG_ERR_EXEC(!b) !!   the LDG-library requested is not GEMDOS 
					 !! executable,
(!B)LDG_BAD_FORMAT(!b) !! the LDG-library requested have a bad format,
(!B)LDG_LIB_LOCKED(!b) !! the LDG-library requested is locked,
(!B)LDG_NOT_FOUND(!b) !!  the LDG-library requested is not found,
(!B)LDG_NO_MEMORY(!b) !!  no more memory,
(!B)LDG_NO_TSR(!b) !!		TSR not installed,
(!B)LDG_BAD_TSR(!b) !!	bad TSR version number (function required
      				!!	not supported by TSR),
(!B)LDG_NOFUNC(!b) !!		ldg_find() has returned NULL.
!end_table

!item [COMPATIBILITY LDG 1.xx] (!ml)
	Some  errors   are  obsolets   with  LDG   2.00:   LDG_NO_MANAGER,
	LDG_TIME_IDLE. With LDG 1.xx,  ldg_error can returns 0 even when an
	error occurs.  In  this case,  the  LDG-manager sends  a  message,
	LDG_ERROR, indicating the error. This feature is now obsolet.
!end_description
######################################################################
!subsubnode ldg_find()
!alias ldg_find
!begin_description

!item [NAME] (!ml)
   ldg_find - get a function inside a dynamical library.
   
!item [SYNOPSIS] (!ml)
   #include <ldg.h>

   (!B)void(!b) *ldg_find( (!B)char(!b) *name, LDG *ldg);

!item [PARAMETERS] (!ml)
         name : name of searched function, (!ml)
          ldg : LDG descriptor, (!ml)
       return : address of the function is found or NULL otherwise.

!item [DESCRIPTION] (!ml)
    This function  looks for  a function  inside a  LDG-library.  This
    function is optimized to find a set of functions in the same order
    they are built inside the library. A good convention is to use the
    alphabetic order.

    If ldg_find() returns NULL, the internal ldg error is set to 
    LDG_NOFUNC. After a set of ldg_find(), as usual in library binding,
    ldg_error() can be called to verify if all is okay.
!end_description
######################################################################
!subsubnode ldg_libpath()
!alias ldg_libpath
!begin_description

!item [NAME] (!ml)
	ldg_libpath	- locate a dynamical library.

!item [SYNOPSIS] (!ml)
   (!B)int(!b) ldg_libpath( (!B)char(!b) *path, (!B)WORD(!b) *gl);

!item [PARAMETERS] (!ml)
            ldg : LDG descriptor returned by ldg_exec() function. (!ml)
         global : global array used by AES functions, (!ml)
         return : 1 if the library is located, 0 else. 

!item [DESCRIPTION] (!ml)
    This  function  returns  the  effective  location  of  a  library.
    ldg_open() uses it  to locate  library. Before the  call, path  is
    filled with the  name of the  library to locate.  At return,  path
    contains the full location of the library. path should pointed  on
    a sufficienly large buffer. This function is different of 
    ldg_getpath() which use to know the exact path of the library 
    inside it.
    
!item [SEE ALSO] (!ml)
	ldg_open(), ldg_getpath().
!end_description
######################################################################
!subsubnode Example of client program

!begin_verbatim
/*
 *  Using the functions of EX_LIB.H
 *	For GemLib user : gem.h should be included before
 *					  ldg.h
 */

#include <gem.h>
#include <ldg.h>
#include <ldg\ex_lib.h>

LDG *ex_lib;    /* LDG descriptor of EX_LIB.LDG */

int main( void) {
   int end = 0;
 
   appl_init ();
 
   /* Load the library and get the functions */
   
   ex_lib = ldg_open ( "ex_lib.ldg", ldg_global);
   if( !ex_lib) {
      form_alert ( 1, "[1][Error with ex_lib.ldg!][OK]");
      appl_exit();
      return 0;
   } 
   init_ex_lib( ex_lib);
 
   while( !end) {
      evnt_mesag( msg);
   }
 
   /* Fin de la lib et du programme */
   ldg_close( ex_ldg, ldg_global);
   appl_exit();
}

/* END OF FILE */
!end_verbatim
 

!subnode Utility functions

!subsubnode ldg_cookie()
!alias ldg_cookie
!begin_description

!item [NAME] (!ml)
	ldg_cookie - find a cookie in cookie jar.

!item [SYNOPSIS] (!ml)
    (!B)int(!b) ldg_cookie( (!B)long(!b) cookie, (!B)long(!b) *value);

!item [PARAMETERS] (!ml)
        cookie : cookie identificator (eg 'LDGM'), (!ml)
         value : cookie value.

!item [DESCRIPTION] (!ml)
    returns TRUE if a cookie 'cookie' is in memory and provide
    the cookie value.
!end_description
######################################################################
!subsubnode ldg_callback()
!alias ldg_callback
!begin_description

!item [NAME] (!ml)
	ldg_callback - invoke an "external" __CDECL function

!item [SYNOPSIS] (!ml)
    (!B)long(!b) ldg_callback( void *f, ...);

!item [PARAMETERS] (!ml)
	f : a __CDECL function
	... : the parameters of 'f'

!item [DESCRIPTION] (!ml)
	This function will save A2/D2 registers, invoke the CDECL function
	'f'	with the paramters given in place of '...', and restore  A2/D2
	registers.
	When a branchements are performed from a code  compiled  with  one
	compiler to a code compiled with another compiler, this function 
	may be used instead of calling directly f(...), because scratch
	register differs from a compiler to another.
	A user case of this is callback functions, where the code invoking
	the callback function is part of a LDG, and the callback function
	is part of another LDG or part of an application.... or vice versa.
!end_description
###################################################################### 
!subsubnode ldg_debug()
!alias ldg_debug
!begin_description

!item [NAME] (!ml)
	ldg_debug - debug a LDG library

!item [SYNOPSIS] (!ml)
    int ldg_debug( char *fmt, ...);

!item [PARAMETERS] (!ml)
        fmt : a string format like printf()

!item [DESCRIPTION] (!ml)
    This function writes a message into  a log file. The complet  path
    of the log file is specified by the environ variable LDG_DEBUG. If 
    this variable is not defined, the function is unactived. If the 
    string begin by a '[' character , the string will be displayed in 
    a GEM alert box instead of be written in a log file.  Otherwise
    this function has the same usage than the printf() function.
!end_description
######################################################################
!subsubnode ldg_cpush()
!alias ldg_cpush
!begin_description

!item [NAME] (!ml)
	ldg_cpush - flush the cache processor

!item [SYNOPSIS] (!ml)
    (!B)void(!b) ldg_cpush( (!B)void(!b));

!item [DESCRIPTION] (!ml)
	This function flushes the caches of processor. It is 
	only usefull with 68040 and 68080 processors which
	have a large cache. ldg_open() flushes the caches
	before loading a library.
!end_description
######################################################################
!subsubnode ldg_Malloc()
!alias ldg_Malloc
!begin_description

!item [SYNOPSIS] (!ml)
    (!B)void(!b) *ldg_Malloc( (!B)long(!b) size);

!item [DESCRIPTION] (!ml)
    This function reserves memory shareable to the operating
    system.
!end_description 
######################################################################
!subsubnode ldg_Calloc()
!alias ldg_Calloc
!begin_description

!item [SYNOPSIS] (!ml)
    void *ldg_Calloc( long count, long size);
           
!item [DESCRIPTION] (!ml)
    This function reserves memory shareable and initializes it
    to zero.
!end_description
######################################################################
!subsubnode ldg_Realloc()
!alias ldg_Realloc
!begin_description

!item [SYNOPSIS] (!ml)
    void *ldg_Realloc( void *oldblk, long oldsize, long newsize);

!item [DESCRIPTION] (!ml)
    This function reallocate a new size of memory. The old
    size must be specified (in oldsize).
!end_description
######################################################################
!subsubnode ldg_Free()
!alias ldg_Free
!begin_description

!item [SYNOPSIS] (!ml)
    int ldg_Free( void *adr);
    
!item [DESCRIPTION] (!ml)
    Memory reserved with ldg_Malloc() or ldg_Calloc() can be
    released using this function.
!end_description
######################################################################
!subnode Calling AES-functions from a LDG-library

If you want to call the AES functions inside a LDG-library, it is  not
possible to use the  standard GEM C-library  of your compiler  because
the AES functions binding use  a local array variable containing  some
importants values  for  AES. But,  it  is  possible to  call  the  AES
functions  using  the   C-library  MT_AES,  provided   with  the   LDG
distribution. A MT_AES call is the same than the AES call excepted one
parameter. This  parameter is  the  AES array.  Keep  in mind  that  a
LDG-function executed  by a  client is  seen like  a function  of  the
client. So  the  client  initiates a  AES  session,  with  appl_init()
function and the LDG-function calls a MT_AES function, using the array
parameter created by  appl_init(). The  first call  of the  ldg_exec()
function initialized a global variable,  ldg_global, that is an  array
of WORD and should  be used to call  the MT_AES functions. Thus,  your
LDG-function should receved as parameter this variable.

Here a small example :

    In the client :
!begin_verbatim
    #include <gem.h>
	#include <ldg.h>

    int main( void) {
        LDG *a_lib;
        VOID CDECL (*a_function)( WORD*);

        appl_init();
        a_lib = ldg_open( "A_LIB.LDG", ldg_global);
        a_function = ldg_find( "a_function", a_lib);
        
        (*a_function)( ldg_global);
 
        ...   
    }
!end_verbatim
    In the library :
!begin_verbatim
	#include <ldg.h>
    #include <mt_aes.h>

    VOID CDECL a_function( WORD *gl) {
        mt_form_alert( 1, "[1][A stupid function!][OK]", gl);
    }
!end_verbatim


!subnode Other languages

If you  use  others language  than  PureC,  GnuC or  SozobonC,  it  is
possible to use the LDG-protocol as  client. Your language have to  be
able to read  the cookies of  the system. The  LDG TSR  (AUTO\LDG.PRG)
installs in the cookiejar a set of functions to load and unload a  LDG
library. This cookies is available from LDG version 1.00. It has the 
identifier  'LDGM' (or 0x4C44474D)  and points to the following 
memory structure :


!begin_table [|lllll|] !hline
Offset !! Size !! Name  !! version !!       Purpose
!hline
0x00  !!  2   !!  version   !! 0x0100 !!   version of the cookie
0x02  !!  128 !!  path      !! 0x0100 !!   path of the LDG folder
0x82  !!  2   !!  garbage   !! 0x0100 !!   auto garbage collecting time
0x84  !!  2   !!  idle      !! 0x0100 !!   obsolet variable with LDG 2.xx
!hline
0x88  !!  4   !!  ldg_open  !! 0x0200 !!   address of function ldg_open()
0x8C  !!  4   !!  ldg_close !! 0x0200 !!   address of function ldg_close()
0x90  !!  4   !!  ldg_find  !! 0x0200 !!   address of function ldg_find()
!hline
0x94  !!  4   !!  ldg_open_ev !! 0x0210 !! obsolet function with LDG 2.xx, 
	!!		!!				!!			!!	now identic to ldg_open
0x98  !!  2   !!  error       !! 0x0210 !! last error occuring, used by 
	!!      !!              !!          !! ldg_error()
!hline
0x9C  !!  4   !!  ldg_garbage !! 0x0220 !! reserved
0xA0  !!  4   !!  ldg_reset   !! 0x0220 !! reserved
0xA4  !!  4   !!  internal    !! 0x0220 !! reserved
0xA8  !!  4   !!  ldg_libpath !! 0x0220 !! address of function ldg_libpath()
!hline
!end_table

The column (!B)version(!b) describes the minimal value of the version
field for which the field is available.

If you want create a LDG library with a compiler not supported,
please contact us.


!subnode Programming advices
The LDG principles come from many constations:

!begin_itemize
!item  How shared between several application a same function built  using
   different compiler and programming language ?

!item  How extended easy the operating system, using GEM, without trap any
   functions or vector exceptions ?

!item   How  a  software  could   enhanced  its  capability  without   new
   compilation (the  plugin  method)  and using  a  special  processor
   feature like PPC on MagiC Mac (who will create a mathematic library
   using PPC)?

!item  How keeps,  with a  such system, a  good performance  compare to  a
   standard application ?

!item  How deals with the 16 bits and 32 bits mode of the Gnu C Compiler ?
!end_itemize

When you'll write a LDG-library, keep in mind theses motivations !

(!U)Rules to create a LDG-library(!u)

There are severals rules to respect :
!begin_itemize
!item  The functions of the library have to be prototyped as CDECL,

!item  With some compilers, Gcc for  e.g, parameters have a 32 bits  size.
   Use 32-bit size parameters in your function (to be compatible  with
   Gcc 32 bits). However, Gcc can  compile in 16 bits mode and  accept
   16-bit size.

!item The double format can create many problem with different  compiler.
   Often, real data can be encapsuled using a union with a long value:

!begin_verbatim
        typedef union {
            double real;
            long   dummy;
        } Real;
!end_verbatim

	A best solution is to use the LDG 'double' library (written by
	Olivier Landemarre) (see http://ldg.atari.org)

!item Each  LDG function  should  be documented!  Remember that  the  LDG
   format allows to document your fonction inside the library itself.

!item  After compilation,  a LDG-library  should be  loaded in  shareable
   memory. For that purpose,  use the PRGFLAGS  program to change  its
   MiNT protection mode.

!item  Remember your  library may be  shareable by severals  applications.
   Keep careful with global and static variables. If a function cannot
   support a multiple call, it should handle a wait loop.
!end_itemize

(!U)About memory allocation(!u)

!begin_itemize
!item  Memory allocation with the standard C function or with the Malloc()
   GEMDOS function  and exchange  of these  data with  the client  are
   forbiden because  the memory  should be  shareable (specially  with
   MiNT). For  that purpose  you can  use the functions ldg_Malloc(),
	ldg_Realloc(), ldg_Calloc() and ldg_Free()
   provided with the C-library developper.

!item These memory functions  make directly  usage of the  GEMDOS function.  A
	  frequent usage of these functions can dangerously fragment the memory.
	It is more insteresting (and more  efficient) to use the functions of
	the LDG memory manager (MEM.LDG) provided with the LDG distribution.
   
!item  Exchange data  from a client  to a library  is correct if the
       LDG-library is a GEMDOS executable with a MiNT mode set to GLOBAL.
		We provide in the distribution an utility (prgflags) to
		set the flags of GEMDOS executable.

!end_itemize

(!U)A typically example of a LDG library distribution(!u)

If you create and diffuse a LDG-library, the archive file should
have the following structure :

!begin_verbatim
  MYLDGLIB.LZH :
    
    \gemsys\ldg\myldglib.ldg  ; The LDG library
    \include\ldg\myldglib.h   ; The C header containing the prototype
                              ; of the functions of MYLDGLIB.LDG
    \lib\myldglib.c           ; Binding of the functions in portable C
                              ; You can provide compiled library too.
    \man\man3l\myldglib.3l    ; manuel page nroff format
    \hyp\myldglib.hyp         ; hypertext documentation of the library
    \readme                   ; details for installation 
    \install                  ; if possible a script or an installer program
!end_verbatim

By convention,  the C-header  file containing  the definition  of  the
function of a LDG-library should be  placed in a subdirectory of  your
include  folder.  These  file  are  included  by  client  using  these
libraries. The C-library  (here MYLDGLIB.LIB  or LYLDGLIB.O)  contains
the  binding  of  the   functions.  It  is   usally  a  function   (eg
init_myldglib()) initializing  the LDG  functions using  the  function
ldg_find(). This file should be linked with the client.

If you create and diffuse a LDG-library, please contact us and we will
create a link from our LDG internet site.

Have fun !

